        "00c94e94-dcd9-4653-84ea-c9fe291d14eb"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "Now that our web page has a name, it's time to add some actual content about your awesome pet fish! Remember, everything you want to *see* on your web page goes inside the `<body>` tags in your `index.html` file.\r\n\r\nWe'll use three new HTML tags to structure our text:\r\n*   `<h1>` for the main heading (like the biggest title on a poster).\r\n*   `<h2>` for a subheading (a smaller title to introduce a section).\r\n*   `<p>` for paragraphs (where you put all your regular text).\r\n\r\nHere's what you need to do:\r\n1.  **Open `index.html`**. Locate the `<body>` and `</body>` tags.\r\n2.  Inside the `<body>` tags, add an `<h1>` tag. Type a main title for your page, like \"My Pet Fish: [Your Fish's Name]\" or \"All About My Fin-tastic Friend!\".\r\n3.  Below your `<h1>` tag, add an `<h2>` tag for a subheading. Something like \"About My Fish\" or \"Meet [Your Fish's Name]\" would be great.\r\n4.  Finally, add at least two `<p>` tags below your `<h2>`. Inside these paragraph tags, write some cool facts about your pet fish! For example, you could write about its name, species, what it likes to eat, or its funny personality. Make sure to put different ideas in different `<p>` tags.\r\n\r\nWhen you're done, check your preview to see your pet fish's story come to life!",
    "generate_outline": "Students will add an <h1> tag inside the <body> for the main title of their page (e.g., 'My Pet Fish: [Fish Name]'). Then, they should add an <h2> for a subtitle (e.g., 'About My Fish') and at least two <p> tags with descriptive text about their pet fish (e.g., its name, species, personality)."
  },
